# React Standards

## Component Design

- One component = one responsibility. If it does two things, split it.
- Keep components under ~150 lines. If longer, extract sub-components or hooks.
- Separate data-fetching/logic into custom hooks. Keep JSX as "dumb" as possible.
- Use named exports for all components. Default export only for page-level files.

## JSX

- Avoid inline objects/functions in JSX props when they cause unnecessary re-renders in memoized children.

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid
<Table columns={["name", "email"]} style={{ padding: 16 }} />;

// ✅ Prefer
const COLUMNS = ["name", "email"];
const tableStyle = { padding: 16 };
<Table columns={COLUMNS} style={tableStyle} />;
```

## State & Hooks

- Lift state only as high as needed — not everything belongs in global state.
- Use `useCallback` and `useMemo` only when you have a concrete performance reason (profiler data), not preemptively.
- When prop drilling goes more than 2 levels deep, suggest Context or a state manager.

## Patterns

- Always handle the 3 async states: loading, error, and empty — not just the happy path.
- Use early returns in components to reduce nesting.

```tsx
// ✅ Guard clauses in components
if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;
if (error) return <ErrorMessage message={error.message} />;
if (!data.length) return <EmptyState />;

return <DataTable rows={data} />;
```

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements: `<button>` for actions, `<a>` for navigation.
- Every `<img>` needs an `alt` attribute.
- Interactive elements must be keyboard accessible.
